# Regression fixture for inc-gv1u: a script `<<` must shift LEFT.
#
# Read by tools/check_shift_opcodes.sh.
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. The colour of the light a mote of fire casts. The
# creature's own script (lib/mon3.irh, the "mote of fire" entity) gives it an
# FI_LIGHT field at birth, glyphed GLYPH_VALUE(GLYPH_FLOOR2, RED). That macro
# packs the colour into the glyph by shifting it left twelve places, and
# src/Light.cpp (ScanFields) reads the colour back out of that glyph to decide
# what colour the light source is.
#
# ON A BUILD WITHOUT THE FIX the shift went the wrong way, the colour was lost,
# and the field was stored as a bare glyph 324 -- colour 0, which is black. The
# light probe recorded the source with colour 0 0 0 and it lit nothing.
#
# WHY A MOTE OF FIRE. It is the cheapest creature in the game whose own script
# creates a coloured light field, its radius is 1 so the footprint is small,
# and it is summonable from the wizard menu without any equipment or spell.
#
# WHY WIZARD SUMMONING. The field is created on EV_INITIALIZE, at the moment
# the creature comes into being, so any creation path serves. The wizard menu
# is the only one that spends no game time and needs no caster.
#
# Menu letters move when a list changes. Run with the seed the check supplies.

@include chargen.keys

# --- SUMMON THE MOTE ONE SQUARE EAST ----------------------------------------
# 'r' is the Elementals category on the first page of the glyph list; 'y' is
# the mote of fire within it. The check does not trust either letter: it reads
# the light probe, which names the source's colour outright.
w
y
@choose "Monster Summoning"
r
y
RIGHT           # the location cursor starts on the player; one step east
ENTER
@dump:summoned

# Save and quit, so the field is also readable from the save file.
ESC
a
